Yes, this works from the command line. ⚠️ It only adds access, so there is no downtime. Your account likely can't edit IAM (`getIamPolicy` was denied earlier), so run it as an admin, or send the block to one.

```bash
export P=pg-ae-n-app-125609
export CLOUDSDK_API_ENDPOINT_OVERRIDES_SECRETMANAGER=https://secretmanager.me-central2.rep.googleapis.com/

for s in DB_HOST DB_PORT DB_DATABASE DB_USERNAME DB_PASSWORD REDIS_HOST REDIS_PORT REDIS_PASSWORD MAIL_DRIVER MAIL_HOST MAIL_PORT MAIL_USERNAME MAIL_PASSWORD MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS MAIL_FROM_NAME EMAIL_SENDING_ENABLED GCP_BUCKET GCP_FILE_PATH; do
  echo "== CN_STAGING_$s"
  gcloud secrets add-iam-policy-binding CN_STAGING_$s \
    --location=me-central2 --project=$P \
    --member="serviceAccount:sa-cn-backend@pg-ae-n-app-125609.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
    --role="roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor" \
    --condition=None --quiet >/dev/null && echo OK
done
```

**Verify.** Re-run the check loop from my last message. All 18 should print `1`.

**If it fails with `PERMISSION_DENIED`:** your account lacks `secretmanager.secrets.setIamPolicy`. Send the block above to the admin who created the service accounts.

**Rollback:** replace `add-iam-policy-binding` with `remove-iam-policy-binding` in the same loop, keeping the other flags.

After all 18 show `1`, re-run the backend build.
